\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite adelic class sets}

\p{A restricted tuple of local transformations can be changed on
the left by one rational transformation and on the right by a
tuple in the chosen reference subgroups. The resulting equivalence
classes depend on which of O, SO or Spin is used at both ends.}

\p{Let #{Q} be nondegenerate on a finite-dimensional rational
space #{V}, with a
\vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C} #{U}.
Use the finite adelic groups of \ref{fcap-004F} and their
\vocabk{rational diagonal subgroups}{fcap-004K}. Define separately

##{\begin{aligned}
\Gamma_O&=\delta_f^O(O(V,Q)), & K_O&=\prod_p U_p^O,\\
\Gamma_{SO}&=\delta_f^{SO}(SO(V,Q)), & K_{SO}&=\prod_p U_p^{SO},\\
\Gamma_{\Spin}&=\delta_f^{\Spin}(\Spin(V,Q)),
& K_{\Spin}&=\prod_p U_p^{\Spin}.
\end{aligned}}

Each #{\Gamma_G} is the actual image subgroup in #{G_f(U)}.
Each #{K_G} is its \vocabk{product reference subgroup}{fcap-004F},
so it is compact and open by the restricted-product construction.
The SO references are #{U_p^{SO}=i_p^{-1}(U_p^O)};
Spin uses its own chosen references and its actual generated carrier.}

\p{For each #{G\in\{O,SO,\Spin\}}, define a relation on
#{G_f(U)} by

##{x\sim_G y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists\gamma\in\Gamma_G\;\exists k\in K_G,\quad y=\gamma xk.}

The \newvocab{finite adelic class sets} are the three quotient sets

##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal C_O(U)&=\Gamma_O\backslash O_f(U)/K_O,\\
\mathcal C_{SO}(U)&=\Gamma_{SO}\backslash SO_f(U)/K_{SO},\\
\mathcal C_{\Spin}(U)&=\Gamma_{\Spin}\backslash\Spin_f(U)/K_{\Spin}.
\end{aligned}}

Here #{\Gamma_G\backslash G_f(U)/K_G} means the set of
equivalence classes for #{\sim_G}, with no quotient topology assigned.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Reflexivity follows from #{x=1x1}. If #{y=\gamma xk}, then
#{x=\gamma^{-1}yk^{-1}}, and the inverses remain in their
respective subgroups, proving symmetry. If also
#{z=\eta y\ell}, where #{\eta\in\Gamma_G} and #{\ell\in K_G},
then associativity gives

##{z=\eta(\gamma xk)\ell=(\eta\gamma)x(k\ell).}

The products #{\eta\gamma\in\Gamma_G} and #{k\ell\in K_G}
give transitivity. Their order matters; neither subgroup is
assumed commutative or normal. This proves the equivalence relation
separately for all three groups.}
}

\p{The class of #{x} is the \newvocab{double coset}
#{\Gamma_GxK_G}. In coordinates, equality of the classes of #{x} and #{y} means

##{y_p=h_p x_p k_p\qquad\text{for every prime }p,}

for one rational #{h\in G(V,Q)} and a tuple
#{(k_p)_p\in\prod_p U_p^G}. In particular, a tuple supported at
one prime #{\ell}, with value #{u\in U_\ell^G} there, represents
the identity class: it already lies in #{K_G}. The left changes
must come from a single rational element; unrelated local left
changes do not define this equivalence.}

\p{The actual products #{K_G} are part of these definitions.
Eventual equality of reference families identifies the ambient
restricted products by \ref{fcap-004F}, but it need not identify
their product reference subgroups. The definition therefore does
not supply a class-set bijection from eventual equality alone.
In dimension zero all three class sets are singletons. These are
quotient-set constructions, without a finiteness, measure or
approximation assertion.}

\p{The finite-stage adelic construction and rational-point
diagonal have classical context in
\citet{Example 2.3, p. 3, and Theorem 3.6, pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic}.
The quotient relation and its elementary verification above are
applied here to the explicit chosen subgroups, rather than
attributed to a class-number theorem. TauCeti's corresponding
definitions are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicOrthogonalDoubleCoset},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpecialOrthogonalDoubleCoset}
and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpinDoubleCoset}.
They use the rational diagonal ranges and the subgroups of tuples
lying in every reference, without assuming that those ranges
are discrete or that the double-coset sets are finite.}
